pthreads: pthread_join waits on a signal of its own, and leaves Ctrl-F to the program

pthread_join (0004) waited for SIGF_PARENT, which is Ctrl-F, and cleared it
each time round its loop. So a Ctrl-F the program had pending, or one sent
to it during the join (a Break from the Shell, another task), was taken by
the join and lost to the program, and woke the Wait early. And every thread
that ended sent Ctrl-F to its parent, so a program that joined a thread
which had already ended, or never joined it, was left with a Ctrl-F that
nobody had sent it.

The joiner now takes a signal of its own with AllocSignal(-1) and records
it in the ThreadInfo (joinmask) with itself (joiner), under thread_sem. A
thread that ends reads both under thread_sem, then signals only its joiner,
with that mask, still in its Forbid(), so the joiner can't run (and free
the signal) before it is sent. The parent is no longer sent Ctrl-F. After
the loop the joiner clears its signal (it is still pending if the thread
ended while the joiner was taking thread_sem), then frees it.

If no signal is free, the joiner falls back to Ctrl-F, as 0004 did, but a
Ctrl-F counts as the thread's only when the thread has really ended (the
loop checks started and finished again under thread_sem). A Ctrl-F that was
pending when the join began, or that woke the Wait while the thread still
ran, is the program's: it is put back with SetSignal when the join returns.
A Ctrl-F the program is sent in the moment the thread ends can't be told
from the thread's, so in that window it is lost.

On daletop, in a lab copy of Instance-32, tests/pthreads/joinsig.c built
-m68020 and -fbaserel32 -m68020 against 0001-0006 passes every case; built
against the stove's library (0001-0004) it fails the six Ctrl-F cases.
t3race and t3w_warm built against 0001-0006 printed "t3 OK" 19 times and
"t3w OK" 18 times out of 19 and 18 runs.

